virology-selection-tutorial-name = Virology: selection
virology-selection-tutorial-tooltip = Learn to design a strain under the selection microscope and hand it over to research.

selection-tut-welcome-bubble = Here you will learn to design your own strains under the microscope.
selection-tut-welcome-chat = The selection microscope turns a blank into a real strain. Designed strains feed the research department and fill cargo bounties, so a virologist has work even without an outbreak.
selection-tut-welcome-tts = Here you will learn to design your own strains under the microscope. They feed research and fill cargo bounties.

selection-tut-take-blank-bubble = Pick up the highlighted strain blank [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-take-blank-chat = A blank is a vial of uncharacterized medium. It decides how many stages you get, how hard the matches are and which symptoms you are offered. Blanks are rare: you buy them through cargo and take them from the virology vendor.
selection-tut-take-blank-tts = Pick up the highlighted strain blank. It decides the number of stages and the difficulty of the matches.

selection-tut-insert-blank-bubble = Insert the blank into the highlighted microscope [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-insert-blank-chat = The microscope only accepts blanks. An ordinary strain vial will not do.
selection-tut-insert-blank-tts = Insert the blank into the microscope.

selection-tut-open-microscope-bubble = Open the microscope [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-open-microscope-chat = Open the machine window. Let us go through it part by part.
selection-tut-open-microscope-tts = Open the microscope window.

selection-tut-explain-sample-bubble = This shows the inserted blank and its stage.
selection-tut-explain-sample-chat = One match under the eyepiece is one stage of the future disease. On the first stage the host gets the symptom you grew first, on the second the next one, and so on.
selection-tut-explain-sample-tts = One match is one stage of the future disease. Symptoms appear in the host in the order you grew them.

selection-tut-explain-reservoir-bubble = This is the reservoir: growth medium and inhibitor.
selection-tut-explain-reservoir-chat = EZ nutrient grows colonies, phenol burns the medium away. Every move costs reagents and the supply is finite: you refill it like an ordinary bottle.
selection-tut-explain-reservoir-tts = The reservoir holds growth medium and inhibitor. Every move costs reagents and the supply has to be refilled.

selection-tut-explain-selector-bubble = Here you choose the symptom you are going to grow.
selection-tut-explain-selector-chat = These are the symptom options for the current stage. The one you pick becomes the target of the match, and another becomes your rival: it can no longer be developed, it only gets in the way. Choose carefully, this decides what the host will suffer from.
selection-tut-explain-selector-tts = Choose a symptom for the stage. It becomes the target of the match, while another option works against you as a rival.

selection-tut-prepare-bubble = Choose a symptom and seed the field [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-prepare-chat = Press the seeding button. It spends a little growth medium and lays two colonies on the field: yours and the rival's.
selection-tut-prepare-tts = Choose a symptom and seed the field.

selection-tut-explain-field-bubble = This is the dish with two colonies: yours is marked with a circle, the rival with a diamond.
selection-tut-explain-field-chat = Your goal is to grow your colony and keep the rival from spreading. It answers every move you make: it claims medium, pushes your cells back along the border, and right before the finish it poisons the medium in front of you.
selection-tut-explain-field-tts = The field holds two colonies: yours marked with a circle, the rival with a diamond. The rival answers every move you make.

selection-tut-nutrient-bubble = Drop growth medium [tutkeybind="UIClick"] and choose a spot on the field.
selection-tut-nutrient-chat = A drop grows both colonies around the point of impact, so do not drop it right on the border: you would be feeding the rival too.
selection-tut-nutrient-tts = Drop growth medium and choose a spot on the field. It grows both colonies around itself.

selection-tut-explain-rival-bubble = The rival answered your move.
selection-tut-explain-rival-chat = You cannot burn it away completely: spores stay in the medium and it sprouts again elsewhere. The goal is not to destroy it but to outgrow it.
selection-tut-explain-rival-tts = The rival cannot be destroyed completely, it sprouts again. The goal is to outgrow it.

selection-tut-inhibitor-bubble = Drop inhibitor [tutkeybind="UIClick"] onto the rival.
selection-tut-inhibitor-chat = Phenol burns out a patch. Burned medium is dead: nothing will grow there again, neither for the rival nor for you. So cut off the rival's path with it, not your own front line.
selection-tut-inhibitor-tts = Drop inhibitor onto the rival. Burned medium is dead for both of you.

selection-tut-forecast-bubble = Run a forecast [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-forecast-chat = A forecast stains the smear and denies the rival its move. Until your next move the drop preview also shows its answer: where it will sprout and where it will poison the medium. You only get two forecasts per match, so save them for decisive moves.
selection-tut-forecast-tts = Run a forecast. It shows the rival's answer, but you only get two per match.

selection-tut-explain-gauge-bubble = This is the match gauge: your colony grows on the left, the rival on the right.
selection-tut-explain-gauge-chat = The mark sits at the cell count the stage needs against the current rival, and it slides right as the rival grows. Pass the mark and the stage can be closed. The gauge tooltip shows the exact numbers.
selection-tut-explain-gauge-tts = The mark shows how many cells you need against the current rival. Pass it and the stage can be closed.

selection-tut-finish-match-bubble = Grow past the mark and finish the match [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-finish-match-chat = A stage only closes if the colony reached the cell count and holds the required share of the field. If the conditions are not met the stage does not develop: you only lose the reagents and can play the match again.
selection-tut-finish-match-tts = Grow past the mark and finish the match. A failure only costs reagents.

selection-tut-explain-genome-bubble = This is the genome: the stages of the future disease you have assembled.
selection-tut-explain-genome-chat = A closed stage can be reinforced with a second symptom, then a third. A reinforcement is a separate, harder match, and the reinforcement budget is shared across the whole genome. Every symptom of a stage shows in the host, so a reinforced stage simply hits harder.
selection-tut-explain-genome-tts = The genome holds the stages of the future disease. A stage can be reinforced with a second symptom in a separate, harder match.

selection-tut-advance-bubble = Close the stage and move on to the next one [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-advance-chat = Reinforcing is optional: the advance button closes the stage as it is and carries the blank forward.
selection-tut-advance-tts = Close the stage and carry the blank forward to the next one.

selection-tut-produce-bubble = Release the strain [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-produce-chat = Releasing consumes the whole blank and gives you a vial with a built-in injector. A designed strain spreads by injection only: a standard microscope does not develop transmission routes.
selection-tut-produce-tts = Release the strain. The blank is consumed and you get a vial with a built-in injector.

selection-tut-take-strain-bubble = Pick up the finished strain [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-take-strain-chat = The lab already knows the symptoms and class of its own strain: they go into the virus database on release, so there is nothing left to work out with analysis.
selection-tut-take-strain-tts = Pick up the finished strain. Its symptoms are already recorded in the virus database.

selection-tut-insert-vessel-bubble = Insert the strain into the highlighted research vessel [tutkeybind="UIClick"].
selection-tut-insert-vessel-chat = The vessel only accepts designed strains. It needs power and a link to a powered research server.
selection-tut-insert-vessel-tts = Insert the strain into the research vessel.

selection-tut-explain-vessel-bubble = The vessel studies the culture and feeds points to the research department.
selection-tut-explain-vessel-chat = Output depends on the strain's strength, and the study budget on its resistance. That budget is shared across every copy of the strain, so running several vessels on the same strain is pointless. If the vessel breaks, the culture escapes as a cloud, so keep anything hot away from it.
selection-tut-explain-vessel-tts = The vessel studies the culture and feeds points to research. A broken vessel releases the culture as a cloud, so be careful.

selection-tut-finish-bubble = Done! You designed your own strain.
selection-tut-finish-chat = You assembled a genome stage by stage, released the strain and handed it to research. The stronger and more resistant the strain, the more points it brings. The same strains fill cargo bounties, and beneficial symptoms from rare blanks grant the host bonuses.
selection-tut-finish-tts = Done. You designed your own strain and handed it to research.

selection-tut-softlock-keep-strain = Do not drop the strain, it has to go into the vessel.
